      "fact": "Political parties recruit candidates, organize leadership selection, formulate programmes and aggregate interests. Selection institutions differ between parties.",
      "applies_to": "Institutional framing only; not evidence for any fictional person or a forecast."
    },
    {
      "id": "uk_parliament_party_roles",
      "url": "https://www.parliament.uk/about/mps-and-lords/members/partysystem/",
      "fact": "Government and opposition parties have distinct parliamentary roles; party leaders and shadow teams organize parliamentary work.",
      "applies_to": "Parliamentary career archetype example; not a universal constitutional rule."
    }
  ],
  "assumptions": [
    "All candidate names, biographies, birthdays, appearances and future careers are invented game content. Coincidental resemblance is unintentional.",
    "Name pools are authored examples, not an exhaustive or demographic representation of any country; bilingual and multiethnic populations require further editorial review.",
    "Existing simulation institutions and ideology supply fictional career themes. This does not forecast real institutions or party survival after the research cutoff.",
    "Four stable candidates per party component become eligible after the cutoff, only through actual gameplay succession. No candidate is a scheduled election winner.",
    "Historical gaps before the cutoff remain unfilled; no fictional candidate may be presented as a historical leader.",
    "Names use authored romanized forms; native spellings are not invented."
  ],
